WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:09.120
On the 3rd of September 2017, the North Korean&nbsp;
military detonated a nuclear weapon underground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:15.280
one that was approximately ten times stronger&nbsp;
than any previous North Korean nuclear weapon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.600
The bomb was so strong the detonation&nbsp;
of it caused a 6.3 magnitude earthquake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.680
The nuclear test was one of several incendiary&nbsp;
actions taken by the North Korean dictator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:36.640
Kim Jong Un, in 2017 and 2018 to demonstrate&nbsp;
his power at home and abroad. Who is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.840
North Korean dictator and how much of a&nbsp;
threat does he pose to the safety of East&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:49.760
Asia? This is the story of Kim Jong Un,&nbsp;
the third ruler of North Korea. 
&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.000
The man known to history as Kim Jong Un&nbsp;
is officially said to have been born on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.560
the 8th of January 1982, presumably in&nbsp;
the city of Pyongyang in North Korea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.440
There is no certainty about this though.&nbsp;
International intelligence services argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:25.120
that it is more likely that he was born in&nbsp;
January 1983 or January 1984. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.160
when he studied abroad in Switzerland&nbsp;
as a young man, his passport gave his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:37.440
birthdate as 1984. It is believed that the&nbsp;
year 1982 was symbolically chosen because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.360
it correlated closely with the seventieth&nbsp;
anniversary of his grandfather’s birth. 
&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:52.320
Kim’s father was Kim Jong ll. He was the son&nbsp;
of Kim Il Sung, the leader of North Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.320
officially known as the Democratic People’s&nbsp;
Republic of Korea. Il Sung became the Supreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.840
Leader of communist North Korea, backed by&nbsp;
the Soviet Union and the Chinese Communists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.840
directly after the end of the Second World&nbsp;
War. He would rule North Korea as its absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:17.920
dictator down to his death in 1994, when he was&nbsp;
succeeded by Kim’s father, Kim Jong Il. Hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:24.080
over three dynasties the Kim family have ruled&nbsp;
North Korea with an iron grip since the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:31.280
Second World War. However, the nature of the state&nbsp;
and its foreign relations have changed over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:38.640
Kim’s father had overlapping wives between the&nbsp;
late 1970s and early 2000s. Song Hye-rim was his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:45.520
eldest wife. Ko Yong Hui was his second wife, a&nbsp;
former dancer who later became the de-facto first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:53.360
lady of North Korea. Kim Jong Un was born from&nbsp;
this second marriage. He is known to have two full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.040
biological siblings, an older brother who plays&nbsp;
no role in the country’s politics, and a younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:06.640
sister, Kim Yo Jong who plays a considerable role&nbsp;
in the regime. Then there are many half-siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:14.240
one of whom Kim Jong Un has famously clashed with.&nbsp;
There may be other illegitimate siblings. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.760
the secretive nature of North Korean society,&nbsp;
it is difficult to be sure.  
&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:28.800
Kim was born into a relatively new country with a&nbsp;
paradoxically very ancient history. North Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.600
for instance, had only been established as a&nbsp;
separate country following the end of the Second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:40.240
World War in the autumn of 1945. However,&nbsp;
the Korean peninsula is home to an ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.440
culture that has made great contributions&nbsp;
to world history. For instance, some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.960
most important innovations in paper production&nbsp;
and block printing were made in Korea in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:58.000
first millennium before being transmitted across&nbsp;
Eurasia to Europe in medieval times. Later, under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:06.480
the Joseon Dynasty that ruled Korea between 1392&nbsp;
and 1910, Korea’s culture flourished, but it also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:13.360
turned inwards as it became fearful of western&nbsp;
influences, especially the spread of Christianity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.920
Westerners began to refer to it as ‘The Hermit&nbsp;
Kingdom’ in the late nineteenth century. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.840
conquered by Japan early in the twentieth century&nbsp;
and remained a core part of the Empire of Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:32.160
until the end of the Second World War. Following&nbsp;
this it was divided along the 38th Parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:41.280
between a Soviet-backed, communist North Korea&nbsp;
and a US-backed, pro-western South Korea. 
&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:48.160
In a way, North Korea continued to carry the&nbsp;
torch as a Hermit Kingdom after the war. Kim’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.600
grandfather had tried initially to unite Korea&nbsp;
under communist rule through the Korean War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:01.520
between 1950 and 1953. Once that failed, North&nbsp;
Korea turned inwards, becoming a secretive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:07.440
authoritarian state. Where South Korea flourished&nbsp;
to become a modern and emerging technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:14.480
economy, albeit under its own dictators and&nbsp;
strongmen down to the 1980s, North Korea remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:20.720
backwards economically and cut off from much of&nbsp;
the world. Still, until the end of the Cold War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:26.800
North Korea had many allies and its economy&nbsp;
benefited from them. The situation would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:37.200
change in the 1990s and 2000s as recent history&nbsp;
cast a considerable shadow over North Korea. 
&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.440
Although Kim was born in Pyongyang and was part of&nbsp;
the ruling family there, his earliest years, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:49.600
much of his life ever since, was cut off from the&nbsp;
day-to-day realities of ordinary North Koreans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:56.480
The family live in over a dozen plush palaces and&nbsp;
elaborate residences staffed and run by hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:03.520
or even thousands of staff and every whim of the&nbsp;
family was taken care of from Kim’s earliest days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:09.440
Unlike the people of their country, who generally&nbsp;
live in poverty, the Kim family was surrounded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:16.000
western technology and modern amenities. Kim’s&nbsp;
relationship with his mother was reportedly very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:23.360
close, as was that with his sister, Kim Yo Jong.&nbsp;
The latter relationship has endured down to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:30.240
present day and she is one of the people closest&nbsp;
to her brother. Kim Yo Jong is even viewed as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:37.520
potential successor if Kim dies before his own&nbsp;
children come of age. His only known child Kim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:44.480
Ju Ae has also been mooted as his potential&nbsp;
successor although as of 2026 she is thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:51.920
to be only 13 years old. As a young man, Kim’s&nbsp;
primary education took place at the Kumsusan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.920
Palace of the Sun school in the capital, a school&nbsp;
for the elite of the North Korean regime, before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:05.280
being sent to Switzerland. There he attended&nbsp;
the Liebefeld-Steinhölzli Schule under an alias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:10.880
Pak Un, though clearly many locals must have&nbsp;
found the strange entourage which surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:16.880
this ten or eleven-year old child in the town of&nbsp;
Bern to be peculiar. There he learned to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.720
some English and German, and presumably a little&nbsp;
bit of French as well, though Kim has never been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:31.920
known for his gifted educational abilities. 
He returned to North Korea in around 2000 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.120
complete his secondary education. It was in&nbsp;
Switzerland where he seemingly developed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:43.280
fascination with basketball, a strange development&nbsp;
given that basketball is hardly that prestigious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:49.360
in a country where football, skiing and other&nbsp;
sports are much more favoured. Back in North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.880
Korea by the start of the twenty-first century,&nbsp;
Kim had to adjust to the new political climate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:01.360
the country. When he left for Europe years earlier&nbsp;
the country was undergoing a political transition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:07.760
His grandfather, Kim Il Sung, who had ruled for&nbsp;
nearly half a century, died on the 8th of July&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:15.840
1994. Kim Il Sung had ruled over a country that&nbsp;
was far from isolated on the world stage. He had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:22.000
backers in Russia and China and a host of closes&nbsp;
allies amongst other communist rulers such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.760
Nicolae Ceausescu in Romania and even members&nbsp;
of the Non-Aligned Movement in the Cold War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:33.840
such as India. All of that had changed in&nbsp;
the early 1990s as the Cold War came to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:40.720
end and North Korea became isolated. This was a&nbsp;
development compounded by China’s economic market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:47.520
liberalisation and efforts to move towards the&nbsp;
model of growth pursued by countries like Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:54.880
Singapore, South Korea and Hong Kong&nbsp;
between the 1950s and 1980s. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:01.600
while Kim Jong Il’s succession had been planned&nbsp;
as early as 1980, it was unclear what approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:11.040
he would adopt as the new country’s leader. 
In the end, Kim opted for continuing North Korea’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:18.000
isolationist approach and even deepened it,&nbsp;
seeking economic autonomy. In the short term this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:24.640
was disastrous. The first five years of Kim Jong&nbsp;
Il’s time as leader were characterised by crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:31.600
and famine. As the subsidies that North Korea had&nbsp;
relied on for years from Russia and other Cold War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:39.520
allies dried up, food shortages abounded. Hundreds&nbsp;
of thousands tried to flee the country. Many more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:45.440
perhaps even millions, either starved to&nbsp;
death or experienced severe malnutrition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.640
If Kim Il Sung was guilty of developing an&nbsp;
authoritarian government with strong ties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.400
to the Soviet Union and China, his son quickly&nbsp;
established himself as someone who had little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:02.480
concern for the wellbeing of his own people. Kim&nbsp;
was away in Switzerland, but he must have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:08.880
aware of what was happening at home on some level.&nbsp;
Kim was a child when his father became Supreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:16.320
Leader of North Korea in 1994. He was also a child&nbsp;
of his second marriage and was not viewed as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:23.600
heir in waiting at this early point in affairs.&nbsp;
Instead, his older half-brother from Kim Jong Il’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:31.040
first marriage to Song Hye-rim, Kim Jong-nam was&nbsp;
believed to be the designated successor. Jong-nam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:37.440
was born in 1971 and was consequently entering his&nbsp;
mid-twenties at the time that his father became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:43.680
the new head of state. He had years to further&nbsp;
acclimatise to the position and was viewed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:50.560
some circles within North Korea as a relatively&nbsp;
shrewd individual. However, two major developments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:56.400
occurred after Kim Jong Un returned to North Korea&nbsp;
from his studies in Switzerland which rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:04.160
changed the succession plans. Firstly, fear began&nbsp;
to grow that Jong-nam was a reformer of sorts who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:10.160
was not dedicated to the Juche ideology of&nbsp;
the country. This is a political ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:16.160
similar to Marxist-Leninist thought. It was&nbsp;
developed extensively in the writings of Kim Il&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:23.200
Sung throughout his long reign and elaborated on&nbsp;
further by his son and successor. Secondly, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:30.240
summer of 2001, Kim Jong-nam was arrested in Japan&nbsp;
after travelling there with his family using fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:37.520
passports and under assumed aliases. The purpose&nbsp;
of the trip was to visit Disneyland in Tokyo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:47.120
This did not reflect well on his suitability as&nbsp;
a potential future leader of North Korea. 
&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.640
Owing to the poor judgement displayed by Kim&nbsp;
Jong-nam and the fact that Jong Un was now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:57.120
entering his twenties and beginning to build&nbsp;
up his own faction of supporters at the North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:03.200
Korean political court, a power struggle&nbsp;
emerged in Pyongyang during the 2000s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:08.960
Remember, Kim Jong Il was well into his&nbsp;
fifties when he succeeded his father. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:15.120
his reign was never going to be as long as that&nbsp;
of Kim Il Sung. It was therefore important for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:22.240
the successor to position himself to take over&nbsp;
before Jong Il died. This was especially so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:28.480
given that there were frequent rumours about their&nbsp;
father’s ill health, including one bizarre story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:34.160
that he had died from diabetes in the early&nbsp;
2000s and was being represented on the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:40.880
stage by an impersonator. To cement his growing&nbsp;
claim to succeed his father, despite being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:50.000
much younger son, Jong Un married in 2009 and had&nbsp;
children. His wife was Ri Sol-ju. Little is known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:56.720
about her background, upbringing or political&nbsp;
views, and perhaps that was the goal. Theirs is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:03.280
a seemingly traditional, stable marriage that&nbsp;
has resulted in several children. In contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:10.400
Kim Jong-nam had several wives and mistresses,&nbsp;
many who have lived extensively in Macau and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:17.040
Beijing in China over the years. Their connections&nbsp;
to foreign entities complicated the possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:25.760
of Jong-nam succeeding even further. 
Beyond his wife and family, Kim is known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:31.440
for being a bit of an enigma. He has&nbsp;
been widely satirised in western media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:37.440
on account of this and there are a lot of&nbsp;
genuinely quirky elements to his personality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:43.680
For instance, his love of cheese has allegedly&nbsp;
led to a complex system of importing cheeses from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:50.640
around the world. He is famously scared of flying&nbsp;
and travels across large distances by train. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:57.120
has a strange fascination with western culture.&nbsp;
In Kim Jong Un’s case this manifested itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:05.760
around basketball and he is a keen fan of the NBA.&nbsp;
This is a peculiar family tendency. His father was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:13.120
notorious for his massive collection of western&nbsp;
DVDs. Kim is also understood to potentially have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:20.640
a large fondness for alcohol. Political meetings&nbsp;
can involve drinking strong spirits and rice wine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.480
What this means is that, even at a relatively&nbsp;
young age, Kim is possibly suffering from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:33.040
lot of health problems. He is clearly obese&nbsp;
and is also potentially suffering from gout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:39.440
These known particularities about him aside, there&nbsp;
is an awful lot about Kim that remains unknown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:45.920
today to those outside of North Korea. From as&nbsp;
early as his older brother’s ill-fated Disneyworld&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:54.400
trip in 2001, Kim Jong Il was beginning to groom&nbsp;
Kim Jong Un as his new successor. Insiders within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.400 --> 00:15:00.960
the regime and defectors have stated that Jong Un&nbsp;
was simply viewed as being most like his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:08.160
in temperament and approach. Hence, at the end of&nbsp;
the 2000s Kim Jong Il began bringing his son into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:14.400
government and giving him new responsibilities.&nbsp;
This clearly demonstrated to the powerful North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:20.800
Korean military and other powerbrokers that&nbsp;
Jong Un was the new successor designate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:26.880
would follow his father as third Supreme Leader&nbsp;
of North Korea. They did not have long to wait for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:34.720
this to become an eventuality. In 2009 Kim Jong&nbsp;
Un was officially announced as the successor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:44.960
Two years later, Kim Jong Il died at 70 years&nbsp;
of age on the 17th of December 2011. 
	
&nbsp;

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:52.240
The country that Kim took over in 2011 was facing&nbsp;
many challenges. On account of its strange history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:57.920
and the development of its nuclear weapons system&nbsp;
under his father, the country was deeply isolated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:04.080
on the world stage. It was also already under&nbsp;
crippling economic sanctions and there had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:10.080
been a failure to revive the economy for the&nbsp;
most part. The situation had improved slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:17.120
since the famine of the 1990s, but there were&nbsp;
continuing food shortages. It was also common for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:24.640
electricity to simply be unavailable. Pyongyang&nbsp;
functioned reasonably well, as the capital. Yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:30.560
even there the power could go out for days&nbsp;
at a time. Further away from the capital it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:36.400
was like living in the pre-modern world. In&nbsp;
the cities and towns there were industries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:41.920
though these were very much like early Soviet-era&nbsp;
facilities or those found in Germany at the start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:48.240
of the twentieth century. The average salary&nbsp;
was the equivalent of less than $2,000 a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:56.640
while the overall size of the economy was just $28&nbsp;
billion dollars. Compare this with South Korea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:02.560
Admittedly the population there was approximately&nbsp;
twice the size of North Korea’s, with some 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:09.760
million inhabitants in 2011. However, at slightly&nbsp;
less than two trillion dollars in overall value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:17.600
its economy was sixty times the size of North&nbsp;
Korea’s. Furthermore, what money North Korea had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:24.000
was generally being expended on its military or a&nbsp;
highly corrupt political establishment. This would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:33.200
allow Kim and his family to live in luxury.
 
	
Two things are worth bearing in mind when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:40.080
to Kim Jong Un and his regime. Firstly, there&nbsp;
is the manner in which he has relied on a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:46.400
number of key nations abroad for the regime to&nbsp;
remain buoyant economically and politically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:52.400
Today a lot of the international headlines are&nbsp;
generated by Kim’s alliance with Vladimir Putin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:59.040
a topic which we’ll return to shortly, but for&nbsp;
the very most part North Korea is a de-facto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:06.320
province of the People’s Republic of China. China&nbsp;
accounts for about 90% of North Korea’s trade and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:12.880
economic support and has done so since the end of&nbsp;
the Cold War. It also has ties to other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:18.400
that are deemed to be pariah states within&nbsp;
the global world order such as Afghanistan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:26.800
Syria, Iran, Eritrea and Equatorial Guinea. In&nbsp;
short, under Kim, North Korea has displayed zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:32.400
interest in becoming a more acceptable member of&nbsp;
the international community. It is under heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:37.600
sanctions and is one of the few countries&nbsp;
worldwide which refuses to sign dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:44.080
international treaties on human rights. Finally,&nbsp;
because of its huge reliance on China, Russia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:50.400
Iran, North Korea has no option but to go along&nbsp;
with whatever the foreign policy prerogatives of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:57.040
those nations have been over time. As we will see&nbsp;
very shortly, this has dragged North Korea into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:04.480
its first hot war in seventy years in Ukraine. The&nbsp;
Chinese premier, Xi Jinping, does not like it when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:11.360
North Korea acts unilaterally to fire ballistic&nbsp;
missiles over Japanese airspace. The Chinese are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:18.320
above all, merchants. They want a stable system&nbsp;
in the Pacific that fosters trade and wealth. Kim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:25.200
is often seen as a liability, in much the same&nbsp;
way Beijing is perhaps not delighted about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:33.840
destabilising Russia’s war in Ukraine has been for&nbsp;
the world order and trade in general. 
&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:40.640
There has been one notable effort made during&nbsp;
Kim’s 13+ years as head of North Korea to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:46.080
to reach a rapprochement with the western&nbsp;
world. This concerned negotiations between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:52.720
Kim and the American President Donald Trump back&nbsp;
during his first term as US President. Trump has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:58.560
many detractors and many supporters. Some of the&nbsp;
things he has been particularly anxious to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:04.480
ground around are in the realm of international&nbsp;
affairs. This has had some success when it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:09.440
to the Abraham Accords in the Middle East and&nbsp;
his recent brokering of a peace agreement between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:17.760
Israel and Hamas in October 2025. When Trump first&nbsp;
entered office in 2017, tensions were extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:23.440
high between North Korea and the west, especially&nbsp;
owing to the launching of missiles over Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:30.240
and South Korean airspace. Trump’s administration&nbsp;
organised a summit in Singapore with Kim in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:37.040
mid-June 2018. This was the first meeting, after&nbsp;
seventy years since the division of the Korean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:43.280
Peninsula, during which a US President had met&nbsp;
with a leader of North Korea. The summit met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:49.760
with little tangible success. A second summit was&nbsp;
organised in Hanoi in Vietnam for the following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:57.280
year, but ultimately this diplomacy had virtually&nbsp;
no tangible impact. Under President Joe Biden and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:04.480
Trump’s second term no further progress has been&nbsp;
made towards rapprochement with Kim’s North Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:10.640
in large part because of his alliance with Russia&nbsp;
and distractions with other conflicts.  
&nbsp;

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:18.480
A huge element of Kim’s time as leader of North&nbsp;
Korea has been centred on the country’s nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:24.480
weapons programme. The regime had begun working&nbsp;
towards achieving nuclear power all the way back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:32.560
in the dark days of the Cold War in the 1960s. In&nbsp;
the 1970s the Yongbyon Nuclear Research Centre was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:38.720
set up with Soviet aid. This was meant to be&nbsp;
for the purposes of producing nuclear energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:44.320
not weapons, at a time when Moscow was trying&nbsp;
to convince its allies to sign up to the Treaty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:50.080
on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons.&nbsp;
With Soviet assistance cooling in the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:57.760
1970s and 1980s, the quest for a nuclear weapon&nbsp;
slowed and Kim’s grandfather appears to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:03.280
genuinely decided that co-operation with other&nbsp;
international parties was better than acquiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:09.440
a nuclear deterrent. It was really under Kim’s&nbsp;
father that North Korea went full speed ahead on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:15.600
acquiring a nuclear weapon. It entered into talks&nbsp;
with the US and western nations about freezing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:21.920
its programme in the post-Cold War world, while&nbsp;
simultaneously moving ahead clandestinely with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:28.480
that same programme. In 2003 it officially&nbsp;
withdrew from the Non-Proliferation Treaty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:34.400
and accelerated its drive to finish making a&nbsp;
weapon. The culmination of all this was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:42.480
the regime tested its first ever plutonium-type&nbsp;
nuclear weapon on the 9th of October 2006. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:49.520
yield was small. It was less than one kiloton.&nbsp;
To put this in perspective, the two bombs dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:59.680
on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in 1945 were 15 and 21&nbsp;
kilotons. Regardless, North Korea had successfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:08.080
tested a nuclear weapon. 
Kim’s time as leader of North Korea has involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:14.960
major expansion of the nuclear weapons programme.&nbsp;
The first known nuclear test during his rule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:20.960
North Korea’s third overall, was carried out&nbsp;
in 2013 and was a message to the international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:28.240
community that he intended to continue where his&nbsp;
father had left off. Then came a swift fourth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:37.440
fifth and sixth test in quick succession in 2016&nbsp;
and 2017. The sixth was the most concerning. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:44.560
the previous tests had been gradually increasing&nbsp;
in strength up to roughly ten kilotons, the 2017&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:52.720
test was estimated to have been anywhere between&nbsp;
140 and 250 kilotons, roughly ten times more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:59.920
powerful than the weapons dropped by the US on&nbsp;
Japan in 1945. This was deeply concerning for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.920 --> 00:24:06.480
international community. A bomb this size would&nbsp;
not completely destroy a city as large as Seoul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:12.800
the capital of South Korea, but it would cause&nbsp;
devastating damage, killing hundreds of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:18.400
of people, perhaps even millions and creating&nbsp;
a nuclear half-life that would make the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:25.120
uninhabitable and lead to widespread death from&nbsp;
radiation poisoning as well in its aftermath.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:30.880
Having tested this weapon, North Korea had proved&nbsp;
to the world that it was now an enormous threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:36.880
to its neighbours. On top of this Kim Jong&nbsp;
Un is the North Korean ruler who has really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:42.960
ramped up the country’s drive towards building&nbsp;
more and more advanced ballistic missiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:50.800
ones that can travel thousands of kilometres. In&nbsp;
theory, weapons like the Hwasong-15 and Hwasong-17&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:58.480
ICBMs could reach as far as the West Coast of the&nbsp;
US. They would be intercepted before they did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:05.280
yet the main point remains: if North Korea is&nbsp;
attacked it could quickly devastate South Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:14.160
and potentially Japan too with its weapons.
It is reasonable to say that the North Koreans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:19.760
are not stupid or crazy, though they might like&nbsp;
to appear unpredictable at times for the simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:26.160
reason that unpredictability is a weapon that&nbsp;
can be deployed in international diplomacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:32.080
The reality is that Kim and his advisors have&nbsp;
developed these weapons and continue to carry out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:38.800
tests because they ensure the continuation of the&nbsp;
regime. Simply put, western powers like the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:44.400
States are not attempting to try to overthrow&nbsp;
the North Korean regime. While interception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:49.920
technology is getting better and better all the&nbsp;
time in the twenty-first century, if the North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:56.480
Koreans fired off an Inter-Continental Ballistic&nbsp;
Missile fitted with a nuclear warhead at Seoul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.480 --> 00:26:02.000
it would likely make it through and strike the&nbsp;
city, for the simple reason that it’s so close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:08.560
to North Korea. Therefore, as much as Kim might&nbsp;
appear volatile, there is a strategic rationale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:14.880
to what North Korea has done over the last twenty&nbsp;
years. Nobody is going to try to overthrow the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:21.920
dictatorship here in the way that Saddam Hussein&nbsp;
was overthrown in 2003, or by the manner in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:27.840
which the CIA and other foreign adversaries have&nbsp;
worked to undermine governments in many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:34.080
countries. North Korea’s nuclear deterrent&nbsp;
has therefore meant that Kim can act in ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:40.480
inaccessible to countries without nuclear weapons.&nbsp;
Take the example of the assassination of his older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:48.080
brother, Kim Jong-nam. After he fell out of favour&nbsp;
and was removed from the succession in 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:54.640
Jong-nam was exiled from North Korea. In the&nbsp;
years that followed he moved around repeatedly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.640 --> 00:27:00.000
For instance, he was in Macau for a long spell of&nbsp;
time and was said to have good relations with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:06.320
Chinese government. There were also inferences&nbsp;
that he was in contact with western governments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:10.960
It is believed that he was allowed to fly&nbsp;
back to North Korea for an extremely brief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:17.760
visit to pay his respects when his father died&nbsp;
in 2011. Even though he repeatedly stated he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:24.400
no desire to return to North Korea after this&nbsp;
to try to displace his brother in the 2010s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:29.920
concerns continued surrounding&nbsp;
his actions in Pyongyang. 
&nbsp;

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:37.200
These were intensified by the publication&nbsp;
of a book entitled My Father, Kim Jong Il,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:44.000
And Me by Jong-nam. Kim decided to act after&nbsp;
he determined that his brother was a lingering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:51.200
threat abroad. In a bizarre incident, two women&nbsp;
approached Jong-nam in Kuala Lumpur International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:58.720
Airport in Malaysia on the morning of the 13th&nbsp;
of February 2017 and threw a substance on him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.720 --> 00:28:03.600
They believed they were taking part in&nbsp;
a prank game-show. In reality they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:10.480
applied a VX nerve agent on Jong-nam after being&nbsp;
instructed to do so by North Korean agents who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:16.720
quickly flew out of Malaysia. He died within&nbsp;
20 minutes from the lethal contact. The two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:21.760
women involved were eventually exonerated, as&nbsp;
they were not aware of what they were doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:27.280
Kim Jong Un has never claimed responsibility&nbsp;
for the attack, but it was clearly the North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:33.280
Koreans. It is hard to imagine a government&nbsp;
acting in such a clearly inflammatory way as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.280 --> 00:28:41.520
this on foreign soil without a sizable military&nbsp;
deterrent of the kind North Korea possesses.
 
&nbsp;

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:48.320
It is the typical assumption for many people&nbsp;
that these human rights abuses in North Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:55.120
are solely the result of Kim Jong Un and his iron&nbsp;
grip on power. Conversely, many people question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.120 --> 00:29:01.760
how powerful he really is, or how actively he&nbsp;
controls the government. Questions of this sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:08.400
were especially prevalent when he assumed power&nbsp;
as a young man. Stories focused on his obsession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:15.040
with basketball, playing video games and apparent&nbsp;
fondness for alcohol to suggest that he might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:21.680
be as actively in control of North Korea as the&nbsp;
regime want the outside world to believe. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:27.920
analyse this, we need to look at the structure&nbsp;
of the regime and government in North Korea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:33.360
The state has been controlled at the very top&nbsp;
by the Kim family since the very foundation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:39.760
the country after the Second World War. Kim,&nbsp;
and his father and grandfather before him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:45.520
have all held the official title General Secretary&nbsp;
of the Worker’s Party of Korea. They have all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.520 --> 00:29:52.000
been leaders of the sole political party within&nbsp;
North Korea, which proclaims itself to still be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:58.960
a communist party in line with its establishment&nbsp;
along communist lines after the Second World War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:04.960
The executive organ of the Worker’s Party is the&nbsp;
Politburo, a kind of government cabinet that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:11.440
ministers who, in theory, advise Kim and act&nbsp;
as heads of different government departments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:17.280
Like any country, no matter what kind of system&nbsp;
it operates under, there is an entire system of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:24.160
national, regional and local government. No state&nbsp;
can operate without such a system. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:30.160
while Kim would appear to be the all-powerful&nbsp;
head of North Korea and this is how the regime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:36.640
presents him, there is a functioning political&nbsp;
system and government apparatus in North Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:43.520
at the same time. 
	
Where the real question of the power structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:49.280
within North Korea arises is over the relative&nbsp;
power of the Korean People’s Army and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:55.760
generals at the top of it. Simply put, the North&nbsp;
Korean regime has survived by placing military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.760 --> 00:31:01.760
strength at the very core of its activity. If&nbsp;
North Korea did not have a large military and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:07.360
a nuclear weapons system that poses a threat&nbsp;
to neighbours like South Korea and Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:14.080
the regime would probably not have survived. Yet,&nbsp;
like all dictatorships, the Kim family can only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:20.800
remain in power so long as it retains control over&nbsp;
the military. If a dictator loses the support of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:26.640
the military, he is usually finished. Accordingly,&nbsp;
some international observers have questioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:32.720
whether Kim Jong Un, who can, as we have&nbsp;
seen, come across as an immature and frivolous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:39.360
individual in certain ways, is actually the real&nbsp;
power in North Korea or if a cohort of military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:46.000
generals wield effective power while Kim remains&nbsp;
the figurehead of the regime. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:51.760
Kim Yong Bok is an extremely powerful figure&nbsp;
within the North Korean military who appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:57.600
to wield an enormous amount of influence. He&nbsp;
rose steadily during the latter stages of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:04.720
rule of Kim Il Sung and throughout the rule of&nbsp;
Kim Jong Il. Under Jong Un he has become one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:09.840
the most powerful people in North Korea, sitting&nbsp;
on the Central Committee of the Worker’s Party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:14.080
holding the title of Colonel General in&nbsp;
the military and heading up several of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:19.840
elite units of the Korean People’s Army. Through&nbsp;
these it is estimated that he oversees hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:25.840
of thousands of military personnel. He has been&nbsp;
broadly in charge of the North Korean contribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:31.680
to Russia’s war in Ukraine and is regularly&nbsp;
photographed with Kim and during international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:37.840
summits with allied leaders of the regime like&nbsp;
Vladimir Putin. Many observers question if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:45.360
people like Yong Bok are the real power behind&nbsp;
the metaphorical throne in North Korea. 
&nbsp;

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:52.880
However, there is a lot of evidence to indicate&nbsp;
that Kim really is as wholly in control of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.880 --> 00:33:00.240
regime as he outwardly seems. He has, for example,&nbsp;
ruthlessly demoted people when he thinks that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.240 --> 00:33:06.800
have become too powerful, and on occasion these&nbsp;
individuals and generals have broadly disappeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.800 --> 00:33:14.400
from public view. "This is what happened with&nbsp;
Ri Yong-ho in 2012 only a few months after Kim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:21.520
came to power. Ri rose through the ranks of the&nbsp;
military under Kim's grandfather and father. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.520 --> 00:33:27.600
became the army chief in the final years of&nbsp;
Kim Jong Il's rule. Just six months after Kim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:33.840
Jong Un came to power, Ri was purged from&nbsp;
his positions. It is unclear to this day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.840 --> 00:33:40.160
if he was killed or what his fate was."This&nbsp;
has happened with many potential rivals for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:46.240
power within North Korea. Nor does Kim simply&nbsp;
remove individuals and have them disappeared as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:52.640
soon as he perceives them as a threat. There also&nbsp;
appears to be a pattern of demoting officials and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:52.640 --> 00:34:00.080
rotating them between offices. For instance, Pak&nbsp;
Thae-song rose under Kim Jong Il and continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:06.480
earn promotion under Kim. He became Chairman&nbsp;
of the Supreme People’s Assembly in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:12.400
but then promptly disappeared from&nbsp;
public appearances in 2021. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:18.240
he has since re-emerged and has been made&nbsp;
the Premier of North Korea, a kind of head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:25.760
cabinet. Clearly what Kim is doing here is making&nbsp;
individuals reliant on him but also introducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:32.320
uncertainty into the lives and positions of any&nbsp;
potential rivals. Concurrently, he has promoted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:39.040
allies who owe their positions exclusively to&nbsp;
him. There is a very strong argument that Kim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:47.040
is fully in charge of North Korea. 
One of the reasons why Kim’s control over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:53.200
North Korea has been questioned is owing to some&nbsp;
of the more unusual events of his time in charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.200 --> 00:35:00.480
of the country. Take the example of the visits&nbsp;
of Denis Rodman to North Korea over the years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:06.800
Rodman is one of the most famous and successful&nbsp;
basketball players of all time. He won five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:14.080
NBA championships in the 1990s with the Detroit&nbsp;
Pistons, San Antonio Spurs and Chicago Bulls and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:20.080
was considered to be one of the greatest players&nbsp;
of his era, second only to Michael Jordan. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:26.480
post-playing career has been less salubrious.&nbsp;
Rodman’s issues with alcohol and mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:32.720
struggles are well-documented. They in part&nbsp;
explain his decision to visit North Korea on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:38.800
several occasions to help train the North Korean&nbsp;
basketball team and indulge Kim’s passion for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:46.000
sport as his guest of honour there. During one&nbsp;
of these visits in 2014 Rodman was accompanied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:53.600
by a large entourage and the entire embassy for a&nbsp;
documentary entitled Dennis Rodman’s Big Bang in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:59.120
Pyongyang. The whole thing turned into something&nbsp;
of a dangerous debacle after Rodman appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:04.800
visibly drunk and aggressive during several&nbsp;
state gatherings with North Korean officials and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:10.880
verbally attacked the narrator of the documentary,&nbsp;
the Irish journalist Matt Cooper. The whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:17.680
did not exactly cast Kim and his nation in a&nbsp;
serious light. We see a washed-up and drunken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.680 --> 00:36:24.000
former sports-star as the bizarre guest of honour,&nbsp;
indulging Kim’s hobby in a country with virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:30.480
no experience of any success in professional&nbsp;
or national basketball events. Incidents like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:38.720
this are what make it so hard to decipher who&nbsp;
Kim is and what he is about as a leader.  
&nbsp;

00:36:40.240 --> 00:36:46.400
The Rodman incident should not fool anyone at&nbsp;
the same time. North Korea has long been known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:52.720
for its human rights abuses and the brutal way&nbsp;
it crushes political dissidents. Accurate figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:57.120
are hard to come by but there are clearly&nbsp;
hundreds of thousands of people either in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.120 --> 00:37:03.840
jail or internment camps in North Korea. The&nbsp;
jails are terrible places, rife with disease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.840 --> 00:37:08.240
and often resembling nineteenth-century&nbsp;
prisons from other parts of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:14.240
Slave labour is employed here to produce goods&nbsp;
that turn a profit for the regime. Torture is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:20.000
prominent, and Kim sanctions it, though it should&nbsp;
be acknowledged that these are both longstanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:27.200
issues that preceded his time in power and that&nbsp;
people are filtered in and out regularly. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:33.920
serve as ‘re-education’ camps used to suppress&nbsp;
any form of political dissent. They are part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:40.240
the wider oppressive system of state censorship&nbsp;
and the cult of the leader in North Korea. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:46.000
was built on the same fundamental principles as&nbsp;
the cult of leadership around Joseph Stalin and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:53.120
Mao Zedong in Russia and China at the time when&nbsp;
North Korea came into existence. 
&nbsp;

00:37:54.720 --> 00:38:00.560
Hundreds of huge statues of Kim, his father&nbsp;
and grandfather adorn public places around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:06.160
the country and the state-run news agencies&nbsp;
typically refer to him as ‘Great Leader’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:12.880
‘Beloved Father of the People’ or even the&nbsp;
‘Genius of Creation and Construction’. It is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:19.040
almost impossible to escape the state censored&nbsp;
media. For instance, North Korea runs its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.040 --> 00:38:25.600
internally controlled version of the World Wide&nbsp;
Web known as the Kwangmyong. There are virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:32.160
no loopholes around it to find information from&nbsp;
the wider World Wide Web. Only high-level state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:37.840
officials or a small number of tech-savvy hackers&nbsp;
living near the land border with China are able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:43.360
to get access to the wider internet and its&nbsp;
huge traffic. Equally, telecommunications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:48.560
are controlled by Kim’s government, with a&nbsp;
state-run network called Koryolink running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:55.440
the mobile phone networks. Kim is the member&nbsp;
of the dynasty who has overseen all of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.440 --> 00:39:01.600
The need for such state censorship of this kind&nbsp;
was unnecessary in his grandfather’s time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:07.280
was only emerging as an issue in North Korea&nbsp;
towards the final years of his father’s rule.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:14.640
Kim, to use a simple term, is the digital-age&nbsp;
dictator of the Kim dynasty in North Korea. 
&nbsp;

00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:20.400
Kim’s position within North Korean society is&nbsp;
not all about brute-force authoritarianism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:27.040
a cult of leadership. A certain section of North&nbsp;
Korean society favours him because their lives had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.040 --> 00:39:34.800
improved during his time as ruler of the country&nbsp;
since late 2011. The 1990s and 2000s were tough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:41.520
years for the regime. Conversely, there has been&nbsp;
decent economic growth in the 2010s and parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:48.160
the 2020s. Elements of the kind of western market&nbsp;
liberalisation favoured by China and Vietnam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:54.880
two countries that claim to still be communist,&nbsp;
but which are clearly not, have been introduced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.880 --> 00:40:00.480
Collectivised farms and factories are allowed&nbsp;
to reinvest their profits and workers are given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:06.960
better pay and conditions based on performance.&nbsp;
The average North Korean still lives in poverty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:06.960 --> 00:40:12.720
though not to the same extent as in the final&nbsp;
years of Kim’s grandfather’s rule or his father’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:19.760
seventeen-year reign. This is in stark contrast&nbsp;
to Kim himself and his wife and children, as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:26.880
as the upper brass of the government and military.&nbsp;
Kim is, for instance, a billionaire in real terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:34.160
with many palaces, a fleet of luxury cars, trains,&nbsp;
and huge amounts of disposable assets and cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:34.160 --> 00:40:37.920
reserves. 
 
&nbsp;

00:40:37.920 --> 00:40:44.480
The family are always conscious of the possibility&nbsp;
of regime change, so billions of dollars in assets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.480 --> 00:40:51.360
are held by Kim and his closest allies in banks&nbsp;
in China and Southeast Asia. They also probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:56.880
hold assets in Swiss and Caribbean banks&nbsp;
through third parties and shell accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.880 --> 00:41:03.840
though this is hard to prove. Beyond this, Kim&nbsp;
has operatives who engage in a labyrinthine array&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:10.160
of schemes to bring in money for himself, his&nbsp;
family and his allies. North Korea, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:16.800
is now recognised as one of the biggest players&nbsp;
worldwide when it comes to cryptocurrency scams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:23.680
The benefit it enjoys in this area is that it is a&nbsp;
state rather than an individual. If an individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.680 --> 00:41:29.280
cryptocurrency scam artist robs hundreds of&nbsp;
millions of dollars in crypto assets, they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:29.280 --> 00:41:35.280
avoid detection for a while by moving to places&nbsp;
like Dubai, Myanmar, and the Philippines or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:42.080
somewhere like Serbia and Bulgaria in the Balkans.&nbsp;
However, they can eventually face extradition and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:42.080 --> 00:41:48.960
laws are tightening everywhere. North Korea is an&nbsp;
exception: a place where the ruler himself is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:55.280
scam artist. Some studies suggest the regime has&nbsp;
made several billion dollars in crypto-related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.280 --> 00:42:04.240
scams in recent years, much of which is&nbsp;
funnelled to Kim and his family and allies. 
&nbsp;

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:10.880
Kim, like all world leaders, had to oversee his&nbsp;
state’s management of the Covid-19 pandemic in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.880 --> 00:42:18.640
2020 and 2021. Global understanding of how the&nbsp;
pandemic played out in North Korea is limited.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:24.000
The regime was slow to admit that the virus had&nbsp;
even entered the country or confirm its first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:31.200
cases. The inference was that North Korea remained&nbsp;
miraculously free of the disease until well into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.200 --> 00:42:38.800
the pandemic, despite its proximity to China where&nbsp;
the pandemic originated late in 2019. In a way it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:38.800 --> 00:42:44.000
makes a lot of sense that North Korea would not&nbsp;
have had a lot of the virus circulating in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:50.480
country. It’s an isolated nation, with virtually&nbsp;
zero tourists or business travellers coming in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.480 --> 00:42:58.240
out. Furthermore, its land borders are not exactly&nbsp;
porous. The southern border is a demilitarised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:58.240 --> 00:43:05.200
zone that a person can be shot for trying to cross&nbsp;
over. North Korea was consequently like an island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:12.080
in the way that it could shut itself off from the&nbsp;
world and attempt a zero Covid strategy. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:17.520
is how it tried to present matters, claiming&nbsp;
that the virus-free country was indicative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:24.080
of the success of Kim’s leadership. In reality,&nbsp;
though, the disease was circulating in North Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:30.640
earlier than most other countries. Although Kim’s&nbsp;
government did move extremely quickly to close its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.640 --> 00:43:37.120
borders and limit travel in January 2020, two&nbsp;
months before most of the rest of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.120 --> 00:43:42.560
it was already present in cities like Sinuiju&nbsp;
where the country’s first death took place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:49.600
in February 2020. Information about the disease&nbsp;
trajectory in North Korea was extremely limited.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:56.000
In 2022, by which time the pandemic was dying&nbsp;
down worldwide, the country finally admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.000 --> 00:44:02.960
to the presence of it in the country and that&nbsp;
Kim himself had a fever. The country admits to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:09.760
74 people having died from Covid-19. At the&nbsp;
same time, it stated that over four million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:16.560
experienced what it called ‘fever’. If millions&nbsp;
of people experienced symptoms it is implausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:22.720
that only 74 could have died from it. Given the&nbsp;
known pathology of the virus, it is more plausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.720 --> 00:44:28.960
that tens of thousands of people lost their&nbsp;
lives in North Korea during the pandemic. 
&nbsp;

00:44:28.960 --> 00:44:33.760
 
The Covid-19 Pandemic led to a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:40.960
diplomatic incident between North and South Korea&nbsp;
in the summer of 2020, or at least it contributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:49.360
to it. The Korean War has never ended. A ceasefire&nbsp;
was declared back in July 1953 at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:56.880
conflict and a demilitarized zone was set up along&nbsp;
the 38th Line of Latitude North of the Equator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.880 --> 00:45:02.480
There have been a lot of flare ups here over the&nbsp;
decades, principally owing to North Koreans trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.480 --> 00:45:08.880
to flee to the South. In the first years of Kim’s&nbsp;
rule, it appeared as though things were improving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:14.960
here, despite the routine border skirmishes and&nbsp;
shootings of individuals trying to pass through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:14.960 --> 00:45:21.760
the demilitarized zone. However, a joint liaison&nbsp;
office had been built at Kaesong in an industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:28.000
base here near the border. The Inter-Korean&nbsp;
Liaison Office, as it is formally known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:34.560
was built following a meeting in 2018 between&nbsp;
Kim and his South Korean counterpart at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.560 --> 00:45:40.800
President Moon Jae-in. Unfortunately, despite&nbsp;
millions of US dollars being spent on erecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:47.920
the four-storey building, the office was closed&nbsp;
in late January 2020. It was then destroyed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.920 --> 00:45:55.040
controlled demolition on Kim’s orders on the&nbsp;
16th of June 2020. Covid had apparently put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.040 --> 00:46:04.880
peaceful reunification off the cards again. 
Elsewhere, Kim has strengthened his few remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:04.880 --> 00:46:11.280
international alliances. North Korea has become&nbsp;
a direct ally of Vladimir Putin in his war with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:17.840
Ukraine. That conflict has been underway in&nbsp;
one shape or another since February 2014 after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:17.840 --> 00:46:23.840
the Maidan Revolution in Ukraine overthrew a&nbsp;
pro-Russian leader. While the initial conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:23.840 --> 00:46:29.680
in 2014 involved Russian annexation of the&nbsp;
Crimean Peninsula and a localised war in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.680 --> 00:46:37.040
Donbass region of eastern Ukraine, the war has&nbsp;
expanded since late February 2022. It has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.040 --> 00:46:42.880
costly for Russia in terms of casualties suffered&nbsp;
and Putin is paying extremely large amounts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:42.880 --> 00:46:51.680
money to Russians to get them to fight. In 2024&nbsp;
a new source of cheaper fighting men was found.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:51.680 --> 00:46:57.120
North Korea directly allied with Russia and&nbsp;
thousands of North Korean troops began arriving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:57.120 --> 00:47:03.440
across Eastern Europe to fight the Ukrainians&nbsp;
from October that year. Over 10,000 North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:10.640
Koreans are believed to have fought in Ukraine&nbsp;
down to late 2025. These men earn far less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:10.640 --> 00:47:17.280
their Russian counterparts and are often used as&nbsp;
cannon-fodder on the frontlines. Kim in return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:23.840
has received hundreds of millions of US dollars in&nbsp;
funding from Putin in return for his assistance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.840 --> 00:47:30.720
with hundreds of millions more flowing into North&nbsp;
Korea via soldiers’ wages and so forth. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.720 --> 00:47:37.360
notable shift in international diplomacy too. It&nbsp;
is the first time that North Korea has deployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.360 --> 00:47:43.920
troops extensively to aid an ally in a foreign war&nbsp;
since the armistice in the Korean War was agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:51.360
in 1953. North Korea did provide some logistical&nbsp;
and material support to the North Vietnamese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:58.240
during the long-running Vietnam War in the 1960s&nbsp;
and 1970s, but the deployment of North Korean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.240 --> 00:48:03.560
troops in Ukraine is far more extensive. 
	
 
&nbsp;

00:48:04.480 --> 00:48:10.320
It is all too easy to belittle Kim Jong Un as&nbsp;
a peripheral player in a network of leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:10.320 --> 00:48:16.800
sometimes called the Axis of Evil in the western&nbsp;
media. The view is often of Kim bungling along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:22.560
like a lackey next to figures like Vladimir&nbsp;
Putin and Xi Jinping when they meet in Beijing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:22.560 --> 00:48:30.080
and so forth. And yet this is not entirely the&nbsp;
case. On the 10th of October 2025 the North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:35.440
Korean government held celebrations to mark the&nbsp;
80th anniversary of its movement after the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:35.440 --> 00:48:41.600
of the Second World War. This involved firing&nbsp;
off a new Inter-Continental Ballistic Missile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:41.600 --> 00:48:48.240
the Hwasong-20, the strongest ever launched by&nbsp;
North Korea. There is also mounting evidence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:48.240 --> 00:48:54.800
the country’s efforts to develop AI capabilities&nbsp;
as part of its military arsenal and functions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.800 --> 00:49:01.040
Like Iran, Russia and China, North Korea will&nbsp;
soon dwarf most western nations when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:07.920
to unmanned drone warfare technology. Those who&nbsp;
dismiss the North Korean regime under Kim as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:13.200
bit of a joke are misinformed. Their current&nbsp;
leader might be around for much longer than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:18.320
we think. With a country that is beginning to&nbsp;
develop economically beyond the perilous years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.320 --> 00:49:25.040
of the 1990s and 2000s, it might well begin&nbsp;
to become a much more powerful player again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:30.640
in the politics of the Far East in years to&nbsp;
come. This is especially so when we consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:30.640 --> 00:49:39.200
the instability of South Korea’s politics in&nbsp;
recent times when compared with the North. 
&nbsp;

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:46.800
Kim Jong Un is a very strange leader. He has been&nbsp;
mocked and vilified for over a decade as North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:52.640
Korea’s leader, viewed as a slightly comical&nbsp;
figure who only finds himself in a position of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.640 --> 00:50:00.000
power because of the circumstances of his birth.&nbsp;
Perhaps there is some truth to this. Intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:06.000
services worldwide believe that Kim was lucky in&nbsp;
gaining the position as the third Supreme Leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:11.600
of North Korea. Through the process of elimination&nbsp;
of other male candidates within the family as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:18.000
potential successors, Kim was chosen to succeed&nbsp;
his father. Although international observers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:23.600
continued to wonder if he is little more than&nbsp;
a stooge for some of the more powerful elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.600 --> 00:50:29.280
within the North Korean military and the long&nbsp;
entrenched political elite, there are more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:36.640
enough signs that Kim is actually well and truly&nbsp;
in control of this highly authoritarian regime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.640 --> 00:50:42.240
He either eliminates rivals altogether or else&nbsp;
moves them around within the government in such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:42.240 --> 00:50:48.000
a way which limits their capacity to become&nbsp;
rivals for power. The assassination of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:55.120
own brother in such an audacious way demonstrates&nbsp;
his ruthlessness. Perhaps the most striking thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:55.120 --> 00:51:01.600
of all is the way in which Kim has developed&nbsp;
North Korea into a country with one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:08.560
formidable nuclear and inter-continental ballistic&nbsp;
weapons programmes anywhere on Earth. Simply put,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.560 --> 00:51:15.440
nobody can remove him from power from abroad.&nbsp;
If the US or any other antagonist tried to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:22.160
something, Seoul would risk being devastated&nbsp;
by nuclear warheads, and perhaps Tokyo too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.160 --> 00:51:28.560
No foreign power is likely to take that risk.&nbsp;
Moreover, through his alliances with China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:28.560 --> 00:51:34.160
Iran and Russia, Kim has strong Asian&nbsp;
allies that ensure that the North Korean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:40.080
economy can potentially grow as part of a&nbsp;
new emerging world order in the Far East.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:40.080 --> 00:51:45.280
Western governments might focus on his&nbsp;
peculiar obsessions with basketball,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.280 --> 00:51:51.760
his alleged fondness for alcohol and his&nbsp;
portrayal of himself as an idiot savant. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:51.760 --> 00:51:58.320
presumably informed secret services in the&nbsp;
US, Britain, Australia, Japan, and South Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.320 --> 00:52:05.120
know they are dealing with a far more formidable&nbsp;
enemy than is often suggested in media circles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:10.480
Only time will tell what happens in North&nbsp;
Korea under a ruler who could remain in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:10.480 --> 00:52:16.156
power there for many decades to come. 
Could you say ID-ee-owe SAV-ont
	
&nbsp;

00:52:16.156 --> 00:52:17.680
 
What do you think of Kim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:25.200
Jong Un? Is he really the all-powerful absolutist&nbsp;
dictator of North Korea, or do you think the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:31.760
structure behind the scenes of this secretive&nbsp;
state is more complex than people often imagine?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:31.760 --> 00:52:56.160
Please let us know in the comment section, and in&nbsp;
the meantime, thank you very much for watching.

